<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Girona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Girona is a city in north-eastern Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, at the confluence of the Onyar and Ter rivers. The Roman Gerunda, it surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s forces in 785 and became the seat of a county of the Spanish March, later held by the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. Its medieval Jewish quarter, Gothic cathedral and walls survive, and it withstood several sieges, most famously by the French in 1809.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/girona.jpg" alt="Girona" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patronat de Turisme Costa Brava Pirineu de Girona · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Girona_des_de_l_aire_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
